# What `docker build` must NOT send to the build engine.
#
# `docker build .` packs up this whole folder (the "build context") before it
# reads a single line of the Dockerfile, and `COPY . .` copies from that. Git's
# .gitignore plays no part: only this file filters the context. Without it the
# context was 168.7 MB, and included .env.

# Real or local settings. A file copied into a build is stored in an image
# layer that anyone who pulls the image can read. Leaving every .env file out
# also makes the build argument the ONLY source of VITE_API_URL: a .env or
# .env.production in this folder can't quietly supply a value instead.
.env
.env.*

# Installed on THIS machine, for Windows: native packages (Rolldown, Oxlint,
# Lightning CSS) are Windows binaries here. The image runs `npm ci` for Linux,
# and `COPY . .` comes after it, so without this line it would overwrite the
# Linux install with Windows binaries. Also 180 MB.
node_modules/

# A previous local build. The image builds its own from source.
dist/

# The repository's history: every past version of every file. Nothing in the
# build reads it.
.git/

# Editor, IDE and local tool settings: personal, and nothing the build reads.
.idea/
.vscode/
.claude/
.remember/
*.log
coverage/

# Documentation and screenshots: not imported by the app.
*.md
docs/

# The Docker files themselves. The build never reads them from the context,
# and leaving them out means editing one doesn't invalidate the cached
# `COPY . .` layer. nginx.conf is NOT here: the nginx stage copies it.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
